# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Contact Book — web app configuration
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and adjust. `.env.local` is what `next dev` reads.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ContactBook API origin. Origin ONLY — no trailing slash, and no `/api/v1`: the client
# appends the version prefix itself (see src/lib/api/config.ts), so putting it here produces
# `/api/v1/api/v1/...` and a wall of 404s.
#
# Deliberately NOT prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`. The browser never calls the API directly — every
# request is server-to-server through a Server Component or Server Action — so publishing
# this origin to the client would advertise a surface nothing uses. See docs/INTEGRATION.md.
#
#   staging  https://apistaging.getcontactbook.com
#   local    http://localhost:8000
API_BASE_URL="https://apistaging.getcontactbook.com"

# This web app's own public origin, used for two things that must agree byte for byte:
#   · the QR payload and public card URL — `${NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL}/c/{slug}`
#   · `metadataBase` in the root layout, which resolves every OpenGraph image URL
#
# Public by necessity: `resolve-card.ts` builds the QR payload during render, and Next
# inlines `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` at build time. No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the BROWSER sends API calls
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set to 1 to make the browser call the deployed API directly, so the Network tab shows
# `https://apistaging.getcontactbook.com/api/v1/...` — the real deployed URL — instead of this
# app's own `/api/cb/...` proxy path.
#
# ⚠ BLOCKED until the API sends CORS headers. Measured against staging:
#
#     OPTIONS /api/v1/profile/me   Origin: http://localhost:3000
#     → 204, with access-control-allow-credentials / -headers / -methods
#     → but NO access-control-allow-origin
#
# and in the browser console:
#
#     Access to fetch at 'https://apistaging.getcontactbook.com/api/v1/profile/me'
#     from origin 'http://localhost:3000' has been blocked by CORS policy:
#     No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is present on the requested resource.
#
# Every origin was refused, including the production and staging web hosts — browser access is
# simply not enabled on that deployment.
#
# THE FIX IS ONE ENV VAR, ON THE API: add the web origin to `CORS_ORIGIN`. The API already calls
# `app.enableCors({ origin: allowedOrigins, credentials: true })` and its fallback list already
# includes http://localhost:3000 — so `CORS_ORIGIN` is set on staging to something that excludes
# it. Once fixed, set this to 1 and the deployed URL appears in the Network tab. Nothing else
# changes: same client, same endpoints, same payloads.
#
# Trade-off when on: cookies cannot cross origins, so auth switches to `Authorization: Bearer`
# with the token in sessionStorage — readable by page scripts, unlike the httpOnly cookie the
# proxy uses. That is why this defaults to off.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_DIRECT="0"

# The deployed API origin the browser uses in direct mode. Origin only, no /api/v1.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL="https://apistaging.getcontactbook.com"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the website's two forms send a submission — /waitlist and /contact.
#
# Any endpoint that accepts a JSON POST: a form service, a Zapier or Make hook,
# a Slack incoming webhook, or a real endpoint on the API once one exists. Set
# it and both pages start working with no code change.
#
# LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE, AND THE PAGES ARE HONEST ABOUT IT: /api/leads answers
# 503 and each form shows "we could not record that, email us at ..." with a
# real address, rather than a tick over a submission nobody received.
#
# Payload: { kind: "waitlist" | "enquiry", name, email, phone?, company?,
#            audience?, message?, receivedAt, source: "website" }
# See src/lib/leads.ts.
# LEAD_WEBHOOK_URL=
